{
  "artifact_type": "development_exposed_repo_scale_feature_ceiling",
  "date": "2026-07-29",
  "claim_bearing": false,
  "question": "Is a repository-scale feature -- repository size, file count, test count, anything computable only by checking out the repository -- worth collecting for Heavy/Light prediction?",
  "method": "Any repo-scale feature is a function of the repository, so a hindsight oracle that knows each repository's true Heavy rate upper-bounds every such feature simultaneously. If that oracle cannot beat the command-only arm, no repo-scale feature can, and 213 repository checkouts are not worth performing. Arms are causal prior-rows-only except the two labelled ORACLE, which are analysis-only ceilings and never deployable.",
  "scoring": {
    "theta": 0.16666666666666666,
    "utility": "5*TP - 1*FP",
    "cost_ratio_B_to_C": "5:1 declared by the human"
  },
  "answer": {
    "standalone_repo_scale_feature": "NOT worth collecting",
    "evidence": "The repository-level oracle is beaten by the command-only arm for CPU in both cohorts (SWE100 115 vs 242; SWE277 588 vs 629) and is exactly zero for Disk in both cohorts and for RSS on SWE100, because no repository's Heavy rate reaches theta at all: Disk per-repository Heavy rate peaks at 12.9% and 12.8% against a 1/6 cut. A perfect repository descriptor therefore cannot fire.",
    "actionable_finding_instead": "repository x binary is the productive aggregate and needs no new data. Causal repo_bin beats the command-only arm by 2.3x utility on SWE100 CPU (564 vs 242, precision 81.9%, recall 70.7%) and 2.8x on SWE277 CPU (1790 vs 629, precision 79.9%, recall 73.8%), and it reaches 85% of its own hindsight ceiling on CPU.",
    "per_target_divergence": "CPU and RSS want repository-local aggregation; Disk does not. Disk command-only beats repo_bin in both cohorts (SWE277 706 vs 167), so Disk evidence generalises across repositories while CPU and RSS evidence does not."
  },
